---
import { Picture } from "astro:assets"
import CaseStudy from "@/layouts/case-study.astro"
import { getProject } from "@/data/projects"
import calculatorImg from "@/assets/projects/print-split-calculator/calculator-full.png"

const project = getProject("print-split-calculator")!

const pictureFormats: ("avif" | "webp")[] = ["avif", "webp"]
const pictureWidths = [480, 720, 960]
const pictureSizes = "(min-width: 768px) 560px, 100vw"
---

<CaseStudy
  project={project}
  title="Print Split Calculator"
  seoDescription="A portable web tool for the NCGA print shop that turns originals, copies, press speeds, and setup time into a recommended split across the shop’s printers."
>
  <h2>Overview</h2>
  <p>
    The NCGA print shop runs jobs across several digital printers. Splitting a
    job across more machines finishes it sooner, but every added printer costs
    setup time, so past a certain point another machine isn’t worth it. The
    calculator answers that question from the job’s own numbers: how many
    printers to use, which ones, in what order, and how many copies to send to
    each.
  </p>

  <h2>How It Works</h2>
  <p>
    Each job is entered as originals and copies, which gives total impressions.
    Every printer has a per-minute speed, and printers are set up one after
    another, so each added machine starts a little later than the last. The
    calculator finds the finish time where every chosen printer ends together,
    splits the copies to match, and only adds another printer if it saves more
    than a set threshold.
  </p>

  <div class="split-flow" aria-label="Production data to machine split">
    <div class="split-flow__node">
      <span>Job specs</span>
      <strong>Originals and copies</strong>
    </div>
    <div class="split-flow__connector" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="split-flow__node">
      <span>Shop settings</span>
      <strong>Press speeds, setup time, threshold</strong>
    </div>
    <div class="split-flow__connector" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="split-flow__node">
      <span>Output</span>
      <strong>Printers, order, and copies each</strong>
    </div>
  </div>

  <figure class="case-figure split-figure">
    <Picture
      src={calculatorImg}
      alt="Print Split calculator for a 25-original, 55-copy job. It recommends 3 printers, KM 1, KM 2, and KM 4, finishing in 4 minutes 22 seconds, with 21, 18, and 16 copies to send. The options table shows a fourth printer would save only 36 seconds."
      formats={pictureFormats}
      widths={pictureWidths}
      sizes={pictureSizes}
    />
    <figcaption>
      The built-in example job. A fourth printer would save only 36 seconds,
      under the one-minute threshold, so the recommendation stops at three.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Several Jobs at Once</h2>
  <p>
    When several files run at the same time and there are enough fast printers,
    each file gets its own machines, and any spare printers go to whichever file
    is holding up the shop. The slower printer joins only as a last resort, and
    only if it lowers the shop-wide finish time. With more files than fast
    printers, the largest jobs are scheduled first and printers are shared in
    sequence. Each file is color-tagged, and numbered badges show the order to
    send them.
  </p>

  <h2>Built to Travel</h2>
  <p>
    The whole tool is one self-contained HTML file with no install, no
    dependencies, and no network connection, so it runs in any browser on any
    machine. Printer count, speeds, setup time, and the time-saving threshold
    are all adjustable, with a reset to shop defaults, and printer names follow
    the shop’s real machine numbering.
  </p>

  <h2>Outcome</h2>
  <p>
    The shop uses it when large jobs come in, so they can be split across the
    right printers and finished as quickly as possible.
  </p>
  <p>
    It’s the same kind of logic that drives segmentation and journey decisions
    in marketing automation: turn the data you have into clear rules, then let
    the rules make the call.
  </p>
</CaseStudy>

<style>
  .split-flow {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin: 1.75rem 0;
  }

  .split-flow__node {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 0.375rem;
    padding: 1rem;
    background: color-mix(in oklch, var(--background) 96%, var(--muted));
  }

  .split-flow__node span {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
    color: var(--muted-foreground);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .split-flow__node strong {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  .split-flow__connector {
    width: 1px;
    height: 1.25rem;
    margin-left: 1.25rem;
    background: var(--border);
  }

  .split-figure {
    max-width: 35rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .split-flow {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 2rem minmax(0, 1fr) 2rem minmax(
          0,
          1fr
        );
      align-items: center;
      gap: 0;
    }

    .split-flow__connector {
      width: 100%;
      height: 1px;
      margin-left: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
